harry was back in the clinic for the  third time that month the last two times  multiple blood tests came back normal  but he was concerned and as a medical  student it was my job to figure out why  dr. my blood pressure was up over 133  times this week  Harry's stress can do that it's normal  doctor my sleep cycles  I was only in REM 20% of the night Harry  that's actually the definition of a  perfect sleep but doctor these aches and  pains  what if it's lupus what if it's familial  Mediterranean fever I had never even  heard a familial Mediterranean fever  Harry tracked every piece of data  imaginable from activity level mood  swings caloric intake all using digital  health tools  Harry was no idiot he ran a successful  small company and yet in his quest  towards physical perfection he seemed to  be suffering from an overabundance of  data we have unleashed the most powerful  tool in medicine to date the digitally  empowered patient these patients have  long been searching the internet for  their diseases but now thanks to the  boom of mobile health technology they  are equipped with an arsenal of over  30,000 mobile health apps and 115  wearable sensors there is an app to tell  you that the flu is nearby and another  that will display your heart's  electrical activity on your smartphone  in real time these tools can help  motivate patients to make healthier  choices and may be able to reduce the  two trillion dollars our nation spends  every year on preventable chronic  disease these tools can place an  informed patient at the very center of  the health care system and thereby  increase the odds of an accurate  diagnosis and decrease the odds of  medical mistakes but what happens when a  layperson  becomes overwhelmed by information about  his body the closest analogy I can think  of is medical school where a layperson  is transformed into a medical expert  there's an old joke that when you enter  medical school you begin to contract the  zees of the day this was written about  in the medical literature as early as  the 1960s they called it medical student  Titus I remember a day during my first  year of medical school I was in an  ultrasound class imagine it you were in  a darkroom  your tender underbelly is exposed and  for the first time in your life you have  a unique opportunity to peer inside  yourself at one point I became extremely  anxious because I was convinced that a  certain dark spot on the screen was  cancer and it wasn't until the  instructor returned to reassure me that  it was just my kidney that I was able to  relax  I had these fears because I was  acquiring new information about my body  without the medical context to go with  it but of course that was the whole  point of medical school I was benefiting  by working with mentors who had years of  experience separating the truly sick  from the worried well but patients like  Harry don't have access to that will  these tools create a nation of  hypochondriacs psychosomatic conditions  caused real suffering and the resulting  stress can lead to real physical disease  and what about on the other end of the  spectrum what about patients who become  overly confident and there are a host of  other concerns  what who owns the data who is legally  responsible when disease is uncovered  and what about security last year at a  conference a benevolent hackers showed  that it was possible to hack an insulin  pump from 300 feet away to deliver a  lethal dose of insulin so what should we  do these issues are being debated in  Congress as we speak as patients and as  consumers should we rely solely on the  US Food and Drug Administration to  police this industry it's a big job and  they're pretty far away what about as  the Silicon Valley community of  investors entrepreneurs programmers and  health tech I think maybe we should take  on some of this responsibility upon  ourselves physicians at the beginning of  their training all solemnly take the  Hippocratic oath which includes the  adage to first do no harm health tech  entrepreneurs are never asked to do the  same I  to offer three practical strategies  which can help the health tech  entrepreneur protect patients and do no  harm  strategy 1 synthesize the data  Healthcare suffers from information  chaos patients and physicians must  navigate through erroneous conflicting  missing information they just want to  know how the patient is doing poorly ok  or whoa red so show them red yellow  green keep it simple reduce push  notifications strategy to empower the  patient providing personal health data  without some sort of analysis and  actionable results can be almost  inhumane even something as simple as  providing common causes for common  symptoms can reduce anxiety what's a  common cause for racing heartbeat  caffeine what's a common cause for  twitchy muscles caffeine stress mineral  imbalance and most importantly strategy  3 integrate the expert sometimes  patients cannot or should not be  reassured by a product silk the famous  Silicon Valley venture capitalist Vinod  Khosla believes that health technology  will drastically drastically reduce or  even eliminate the need for physicians  but I disagree I think people will  always want access to the experience the  medical context and frankly the human  element that health care professionals  provide and with technology their power  to heal is even greater and yet this is  the most challenging of the three why  because large electronic medical record  companies keep their digital platforms  mostly closed in part to protect profits  that means that the makers of the health  apps cannot seamlessly integrate into  the medical record systems that the  physicians look at and that means that  the physicians and the patients cannot  easily work together simply because  their tools do not talk to one another  this is the single biggest problem in  health technology and it needs to change  as consumers as patients as physicians  as investors we can put pressure on both  the little health technology  companies as well as the large medical  record companies to integrate their  systems and we can do that through  conversations through advocacy and  through our purchasing decisions health  technology will be at the center of  hundreds of millions of medical  decisions going forward I am asking the  health tech entrepreneur to take on the  role of healer and take on the  Hippocratic oath to do no harm we should  be using business to benefit patients  rather than using patients to benefit  business these businesses should go  beyond providing data they should build  products that truly enhance their  customers well-being products that  provide synthesized actionable data that  both empower the patient and integrate  the expert into a cohesive system these  are the products that will be rewarded  in the marketplace thank you  you
 

